# Lesson 01 -- Magmas, via Rock-Paper-Scissors.
#
# Meaning here: a MAGMA is a set with a closed binary operation.  Nothing else
# is assumed -- no associativity, no identity, no inverses, no order.
# Also called: "groupoid" in older algebra texts -- NOT the category-theory
# groupoid, which is a different object entirely.  See docs/terminology.md.
#
# fight(a, b) returns the winner; a tie returns the shared choice.  That is a
# closed binary operation on {rock, paper, scissors}, so it is a magma -- and
# this lesson shows it is nothing more than a magma.
#
# The same operation is shown three ways: as a function, as a Cayley table,
# and as a colored diagram.  They are one object seen from three sides.
#
# NOT a Web UI file. This is a CLI lesson: it uses include and write_text,
# neither of which the browser session has, and it ends in ok(...) so the
# suite can gate on it -- which means the playground would print "Ok(...)"
# instead of drawing anything. To see these diagrams in the browser, paste
# web/rps_cayley_web.mlpl and web/rps_associativity_web.mlpl.
# See docs/viewing.md.
#
# visual: ascii, svg
# loops: 0
# complexity: n^2 to build the table, n^3 to check associativity

include "../../lib/algebra.mlpl"
include "../../lib/render.mlpl"

u:prolog({
  title: "LESSON 01 -- MAGMAS, via Rock-Paper-Scissors",
  intro: "A magma is a set with one closed binary operation and nothing else. This lesson builds the Rock-Paper-Scissors 'who wins' operation, asks the machine which laws it obeys, and writes two diagrams: a colored Cayley table and an animation of the moment associativity fails."
})

# --- View 1: the operation as a function -----------------------------------
# rock 0 beats scissors 2, paper 1 beats rock 0, scissors 2 beats paper 1,
# so x beats y exactly when (x - y) mod 3 is 1.  The winner of x and y is
# whichever of them the other does not beat.
def u:beats(x, y) { eq(mod(x - y + 3, 3), 1) }
def u:fight(x, y) { if u:beats(x, y) { x } else { y } }

# --- View 2: the same operation as a Cayley table ---------------------------
# table() is the outer product: every pair of choices, evaluated at once.
# One line replaces the nine-row table a textbook prints by hand.
s = {
  title: "Rock-Paper-Scissors (magma)",
  names: ["rock", "paper", "scissors"],
  table: table(:u:fight, u:elements(3), u:elements(3))
}

u:show_table(s)

# The function and the table are the same object; assert it rather than say it.
spot = eq(u:fight(0, 2), take(take(s.table, 0, 0), 0, 2))

print("")
print("Which laws hold?")
u:show_laws(s)

# --- Closed, commutative, and nothing else ---------------------------------
# Closure is what makes this a magma at all: fight never invents a fourth
# choice.  Commutativity holds because the winner does not depend on which
# player moved first.  Associativity fails -- and the failure is exhibited,
# not merely reported.
print("")
x = u:show_assoc_counterexample(s)

# --- View 3: the diagram ----------------------------------------------------
# Color by result element.  Nothing about this table is a Latin square, no
# band reproduces the headings: that is what "magma and no more" looks like.
wrote = u:write_cayley_svg("out/01-rock-paper-scissors.svg", s)

# --- View 4: the game you already know --------------------------------------
# The same operation as a directed graph: a -> b when a * b = a. Read off the
# table, so the drawing cannot disagree with the algebra.
cycle = u:write_dominance_svg("out/01-rock-paper-scissors-cycle.svg",
  {title: "Rock-Paper-Scissors: who beats whom", names: s.names, table: s.table})

# --- View 5: the failure, animated ------------------------------------------
# Two markers walk the two bracketings of the same three elements and land on
# different colors.  SMIL is just more SVG text, so this costs nothing that the
# static diagram did not already cost -- no encoder, no new builtin.
walked = u:write_assoc_walk_svg("out/01-rock-paper-scissors-associativity.svg",
  {title: "Rock-Paper-Scissors: associativity fails", names: s.names, table: s.table})

u:epilog_open()

u:explain("out/01-rock-paper-scissors-cycle.svg",
  "Three circles, one per choice, and an arrow a -> b meaning 'a beats b'.")
u:note("Rock smashes scissors, scissors cuts paper, paper covers rock.")
u:note("WHAT TO NOTICE: the arrows form ONE closed loop with no start and no end. Nothing")
u:note("sits at the top of the order, which is exactly why the game is fair -- and it is")
u:note("also the first hint that this operation cannot be associative, because a cycle has")
u:note("no consistent way to bracket three steps.")

u:explain("out/01-rock-paper-scissors.svg",
  "A 3x3 grid: one square per matchup. Rows and columns both run rock, paper, scissors.")
u:note("Each square is filled with the COLOR OF THE WINNER, and labelled with the winner's name.")
u:note("Blue = rock, red = paper, green = scissors, and the same three colors label the edges.")
u:note("WHAT TO NOTICE: every square holds one of the three -- the operation never invents a")
u:note("fourth outcome. That is CLOSURE, and closure is the only law a magma requires.")
u:note("WHAT IS ABSENT: no row and no column repeats the header order left-to-right, so no")
u:note("element leaves its opponent unchanged -- there is no identity. And no color appears")
u:note("exactly once per row and column, so it is not a Latin square and not a group.")

u:explain("out/01-rock-paper-scissors-associativity.svg",
  "The same grid, ANIMATED, with two rings that move: white and red.")
u:note("Both rings trace one bracketing of rock * paper * scissors. Each pauses on a cell,")
u:note("slides to the cell its result names, and pauses again -- one hop per pair of brackets.")
u:note("WHITE walks (a*b)*c: it starts on (rock, paper), reads paper, then hops to")
u:note("(paper, scissors) and lands on scissors.")
u:note("RED walks a*(b*c): it starts on (paper, scissors), reads scissors, then hops to")
u:note("(rock, scissors) and lands on rock.")
u:note("WHAT TO NOTICE: the two swatches in the legend end up DIFFERENT COLORS. Same three")
u:note("elements, same order, only the brackets moved -- and the answer changed. That is what")
u:note("non-associativity looks like, and it is why the checker returns a witness, not a 'no'.")

u:takeaway("Rock-Paper-Scissors is closed and commutative, and that is all. It is a magma: the bottom rung of the ladder, with no associativity, no identity, and no inverses above it.")

print("wrote out/01-rock-paper-scissors-cycle.svg:", is_ok(cycle))
print("wrote out/01-rock-paper-scissors.svg:", is_ok(wrote))
print("wrote out/01-rock-paper-scissors-associativity.svg:", is_ok(walked))

# --- Invariants -------------------------------------------------------------
c = u:classify(s.table)
laws_ok = c.closed * c.commutative * (1 - c.associative) * (1 - c.identity)
witness_ok = x.found * eq(x.a, 0) * eq(x.b, 1) * eq(x.c, 2)
rung_ok = c.magma * (1 - c.semigroup) * (1 - c.monoid) * (1 - c.group)
if laws_ok * witness_ok * rung_ok * spot * is_ok(wrote) * is_ok(walked) * is_ok(cycle) {
  ok("rock-paper-scissors is a commutative magma and no more")
} else {
  err("rock-paper-scissors invariants failed")
}
